# Line endings.
#
# Windows git defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. For anything executed by a shell that is not cosmetic — bash
# refuses a CRLF script outright:
#
#     $'\r': command not found
#     setup.sh: line 2: syntax error near unexpected token
#
# so setup.sh, install.sh, shutdown.sh and get-mcp-env.sh would all break on a
# default Windows clone before doing anything. Pinning eol=lf here fixes it at
# the source, for every contributor, regardless of their local git config.
#
# The Dockerfile and the container entrypoint are in the same boat: they are
# interpreted inside a Linux image, where a stray CR becomes part of the
# command. Dockerfile.agent also strips CR from entrypoint.sh at build time as
# belt-and-braces, which still matters when an image is built from a working
# tree that was already checked out with CRLF.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf
entrypoint.sh   text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for files read by Linux-side tooling at runtime.
*.json          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Let git decide for ordinary source; these are never executed directly.
*.ts            text
*.js            text
*.css           text
*.html          text
*.md            text

# Binary assets must never be touched.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.gif           binary
*.mp4           binary
*.zip           binary
*.ico           binary
